---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';

const dependencies = `[dependencies]
dotcfg = { version = "0.4.0", features = ["async"] }
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread"] }`;

const singleSource = `use dotcfg::DotCfg;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct AppConfig {
    port: u16,
}

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let cfg = DotCfg::new("myapp");
    let previous: Option<AppConfig> = cfg.load_async().await?;
    cfg.save_async(&AppConfig { port: 8080 }).await?;
    let port: u16 = cfg.get_as_async("port").await?;
    println!("Previous config: {}, current port: {port}", previous.is_some());
    Ok(())
}`;

const layered = `use dotcfg::{ConfigStack, DotCfg};

let defaults = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("./config/defaults");
let project = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("./config/project");
let cfg = ConfigStack::new()
    .layer("defaults", defaults)
    .layer("project", project)
    .write_to("project")
    .with_env_prefix("APP")
    .build()?;

let port: u16 = cfg.get_as_async("server.port").await?;
println!("Current port: {port}");
cfg.set_val_async("server.port", 8080u16).await?;`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Async I/O — dotcfg"
  description="Use Tokio-backed async file operations with DotCfg and ConfigStack."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">Core API</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Async I/O
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      v0.4.0 introduces optional Tokio-backed async counterparts for <code>DotCfg</code> and{' '}
      <code>ConfigStack</code> loading, saving, and key access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/loading-saving">async loading and saving</a>,{' '}
      <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/key-access">async key access</a>, or{' '}
      <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/config-stack">async ConfigStack</a>.
      Each guide links back to the corresponding configuration rules.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Enable the feature
    </h2>
    <CodeBlock label="Cargo.toml" code={dependencies} />
    <p>
      The <code>async</code> feature enables Tokio file I/O inside dotcfg. Your application also
      needs a Tokio runtime to call the methods with <code>.await</code>. TOML remains the default
      format; JSON and YAML can be enabled alongside <code>async</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      One file with DotCfg
    </h2>
    <p>
      Call <code>load_async()</code> when a missing file is acceptable, or{' '}
      <code>load_or_error_async()</code> when it is required.{' '}
      <code>load_or_default_async()</code> writes a default value when the file is missing.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="src/main.rs" code={singleSource} />
    <p>
      For individual keys, use <code>get_async()</code> and <code>set_async()</code> for strings,
      or <code>get_as_async()</code> and <code>set_val_async()</code> for typed values.{' '}
      <code>save_async()</code> writes a complete config. Async file discovery, existence checks,
      and deletion are also available on <code>DotCfg</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/loading-saving">async loading and saving</a>{' '}
      for missing files and complete structs, then <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/key-access">async key access</a>{' '}
      for strings, typed values, and nested keys.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Layered files with ConfigStack
    </h2>
    <p>
      Build the stack just as you would for synchronous use. Async reads still merge earlier and
      later layers, with environment overrides on top. Async writes still go only to the source
      selected by <code>write_to()</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust, inside an async function" code={layered} />
    <p>
      <code>ConfigStack</code> also has <code>get_async()</code>, <code>load_async()</code>,{' '}
      <code>load_or_error_async()</code>, <code>set_async()</code>, and <code>save_async()</code>.
      It has no <code>load_or_default_async()</code>: the stack does not choose a file to create
      when every source is missing. To work with one named source directly, call{' '}
      <code>cfg.source("project")?</code> and then a <code>DotCfg</code> async method.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/async-io/config-stack">async ConfigStack guide</a>{' '}
      covers resolved reads, write targets, and direct source access.
    </p>
    <Callout type="info" title="Same configuration rules">
      <p>
        Async methods change how files are read and written. Parsing, merging, environment
        precedence, missing-file behavior, and write ownership follow the same rules as the sync
        methods. See <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/config-stack/resolution">layers and resolution</a>{' '}
        for those rules.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
